alfe needs to pass an empty dwg to bricscad and autocad.  To avoid
reusing previous empty.dwg files, that may be modified or locked by
other instances alfe must create a new empty.dwg file for each
invocation of the CAD, in the invocation workdir.  Eventually we
should have resources and documents and templates, in
/opt/local/share/clautolisp/*/ but for alfe it may be preferable to
keep it stand alone, and since empty.dwg is small, to include it as a
resource in the lisp image.

The following can be used load the empty.dwg file contents into a
vector kept in the lisp image, and to save a copy into a new file when
and where it will be needed. (adjust the "empty.dwg" path for
load-time reading when compiling the source file.

Move the empty.dwg file from issues/open to the right directory
(eg. along with the sources that load it).



#+begin_src lisp
    (defvar *empty-dwg-contents*
      (load-time-value
       (let ((sequence))
         (with-open-file (in "empty.dwg"  :element-type '(unsigned-byte 8))
           (read-sequence (setf sequence (make-array (file-length in)
                                                     :element-type '(unsigned-byte 8)))
                          in))
         sequence)))

    (defun save-empty-dwg (path)
      (with-open-file (out path :element-type '(unsigned-byte 8)
                           :direction :output
                                :if-exists :supersede
                                :if-does-not-exist :create)
        (write-sequence *empty-dwg-contents* out)))
#+end_src


## IMPLEMENTED (2026-08-30)

alfe carries the empty drawing in its image and writes a FRESH copy into
each invocation's workdir.

  autolisp-front-end/source/empty-drawing.lisp   the resource + API
  autolisp-front-end/source/empty.dwg            the file, beside its loader
                                                 (AC1032, 13733 bytes)

*EMPTY-DWG-CONTENTS*, SAVE-EMPTY-DWG and FRESH-EMPTY-DWG, as sketched in
this ticket. One departure from the sketch, and it matters: the bytes are
read by a MACRO at COMPILE time, not by LOAD-TIME-VALUE. LOAD-TIME-VALUE
runs when the FASL is loaded, and at that moment *LOAD-TRUENAME* names
the fasl in the ASDF cache rather than the source directory, so the
relative "empty.dwg" would not resolve. A macro expands while
*COMPILE-FILE-TRUENAME* still points at the source, and the literal
vector lands in the fasl and then in the dumped image, which is what was
wanted.

WIRED INTO BOTH BACKENDS, at the same priority in each: an explicitly
named drawing wins (--dwg, $AUTOLISP_DWG, $AUTOLISP_BRICSCAD_TEMPLATE),
then the fresh copy, then the vendor templates as a safety net.

  DISCOVER-BRICSCAD-TEMPLATE  gained :workdir
  DISCOVER-AUTOCAD-TEMPLATE   gained :workdir

This supersedes the argument that stood in backend-bricscad.lisp against
committing a .dwg ("a vendor binary bound to a format version"). The
objection is real and is recorded where the resource lives: a future
engine refusing AC1032 refuses it LOUDLY, and the file is one to replace
rather than maintain.

## The shared file had to be removed from the CALLERS too

Wiring alfe was not enough: the CI jobs and scripts NAMED the shared
drawing, and an explicit name still wins.

  .gitlab/native.yml          PROBE_DWG dropped (4 jobs), and -Dwg with it
  run-vendor-probes.ps1       -Dwg default was the shared path; now empty
  run-path-sysvars-probe.ps1  its hand-rolled per-job COPY of the shared
                              file removed -- alfe does this for every
                              caller now, instead of once per script
  verify-bricscad-hidden-ui.ps1  no longer defaults AUTOLISP_DWG
  run-vendor-probes.sh        comment corrected: we DO ship a drawing now

run-path-sysvars-probe.ps1's comment is worth keeping in mind: it had
already met this exact failure ("already in use, open read-only?",
invisible under /Automation, holding the concurrency-1 CAD runner until
timeout -- cad-runner-wedged-by-modal-dialog.issue) and solved it locally.
One script solving it locally is why it kept happening everywhere else.

## Tests

autolisp-front-end/tests/empty-drawing-tests.lisp: the resource survives
into the image and is not a zero-length vector (the failure mode where
everything still `works' and the CAD is handed an empty file); the header
is AC1032; two invocations get DIFFERENT files; a copy is byte-identical;
a fresh write replaces a partial file left by a crash; an unwritable
directory declines with NIL rather than failing the run.

One existing test changed: the AutoCAD batch argv test asserted that argv
named the SHIPPED acadiso.dwt and that :NO-DWG was signalled when no
template existed anywhere. Neither holds now, by design -- it asserts
instead that the drawing handed to the CAD is IN THIS RUN'S WORKDIR,
which is the property that matters.

